1	// pattern: Mixed (Functional Core types + guard/converters; Imperative Shell commands)
2	//
3	// Functional Core: MigrationInputs, SignedMigrationOp, MigrateError, the strict
4	//                  pre-sign guard, the RecommendedCredentials -> typed-map
5	//                  converters, current-state extraction, and diff computation
6	//                  (all pure).
7	// Imperative Shell: build_migration_op / submit_migration_op (network + Keychain +
8	//                   signing) and their Tauri command wrappers.
9	//
10	// This is the wallet-authorized (self-signed) identity leg of account migration
11	// (ADR-0002, path 1). The DID-repointing PLC operation is built and signed LOCALLY
12	// with the per-DID device key and submitted directly to plc.directory — no email
13	// token, no signPlcOperation round-trip. It is applicable whenever the wallet holds
14	// an authorized key in the DID's current rotationKeys; otherwise it refuses and the
15	// migration orchestrator falls back to the PDS-signed interop path.
16	//
17	// Contrast with claim.rs: a claim must change NOTHING but insert the device key at
18	// rotationKeys[0], so its guard rejects every key/service mutation. A migration is
19	// the inverse — it MUST rewrite services.atproto_pds, rotationKeys[1], and
20	// verificationMethods.atproto to the destination's values. The one invariant that
21	// must survive is rotationKeys[0] == the wallet device key; that single fact is the
22	// entire "credible exit" guarantee (and any op the user did not initiate is caught
23	// by plc_monitor and reversible via recovery.rs).
24	
